  <!-- ============ CONTROLS ============ -->
  <section id="controls" aria-labelledby="controls-h">
    <h2 id="controls-h">Controls</h2>
    <p>Listed in signal order. Numbers follow the Versio panel: by column, top to bottom. Every knob has a CV input with the same number, which adds to the knob's position.</p>
    <div class="ref">
      <article><span class="num">1</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Predelay</h3><span class="range">0–500 ms · free-running</span></div>
        <p>How long after the kick the rumble starts. Pushing the swell later moves it off the kick and into the gap before the next one.</p>
        <ul><li>Linear: noon is 250 ms, and each 1% of the knob is 5 ms, so note values spread evenly. At 130 BPM an eighth is near noon (46%) and a quarter near the top (92%).</li><li>500 ms is a quarter note at 120 BPM, so every note value up to a quarter fits from 120 BPM up. Not synced to tempo: dial it by ear.</li><li>Smoothed, so turning it doesn't zipper.</li></ul>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">2</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Diffusion</h3><span class="range">sparse → dense</span></div>
        <p>How spread out the early reflections are, before the smear. Shindo adds a few delayed copies of the kick tens of milliseconds apart, the way a reverb's early section does, and the knob sets their pattern.</p>
        <ul><li>Left: four regular taps every 14 ms. Distinct doubles of the kick, and a deep comb across the sub that moves the body's tone.</li><li>Right: eight scattered taps over 3–60 ms. A smooth thickening.</li><li>Level stays put as the knob turns; only the pattern changes. Easiest to hear with a short Smear, since a long wash smooths the comb away.</li></ul>
        <p class="note"><span class="todo">Being decided</span> Whether noon should be dry, with sparse taps fading in to the left and dense ones to the right.</p>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">5</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Smear</h3><span class="range">size + decay, one macro</span></div>
        <p>How big and how long the cloud is. Low settings give a short, tight bloom; high settings a slow, wide swell that rings out between bars.</p>
        <ul><li>Dark FDN engines: the space grows mostly in the top half of the knob, and the decay runs from about a quarter of a second to about 5 seconds.</li><li>Long tails stack up across kicks, so the output is trimmed a little as Smear rises: Smear changes how long it rings, not how loud it is.</li></ul>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">3</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Drive</h3><span class="range">clean → obliterate · per output</span></div>
        <p>Saturation, with the type set by switch 9. Each output's band is driven on its own after the split, so the sub saturates as a sub on Out 1 and keeps its own harmonics there, while the texture saturates as texture on Out 2.</p>
        <ul><li>Clean at the bottom: the curve fades in over the first 15% of the knob, so the type switch does nothing at 0.</li><li>Transistor and Iron reach ×50 at the top, about ×12 at noon; Tube stops at ×10, where its even harmonics live.</li><li>Loud parts get denser, quiet parts stay as they are: Drive changes character, not loudness, until the top of the knob.</li></ul>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">6</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Split</h3><span class="range">40 Hz – 2 kHz · 24 dB/oct crossover</span></div>
        <p>Where the sound divides between the two outputs. Below the split is the rumble, on Out 1; above it is the texture, on Out 2. Noon is 140 Hz, near the usual top of a rumble.</p>
        <ul><li>The two halves add back up to the whole sound, so Out 1 and Out 2 mixed at equal level sound like one unsplit rumble.</li><li>Turn it down for a tighter, darker rumble and more texture; turn it up to put more of the sound on Out 1, including Drive's harmonics.</li></ul>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">4</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Texture duck</h3><span class="range">0–100%</span></div>
        <p>How much Out 2 pumps with the rumble. At 0 the texture rides straight through the kick. Turn it up and the texture drops with the rumble on every hit and swells back at the same Release.</p>
        <ul><li>Out 1 always ducks fully: the kick and the rumble share the sub, so the rumble drops out completely on every hit. The texture is where a depth control makes sense.</li></ul>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">7</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Duck release</h3><span class="range">20 ms – 1 s</span></div>
        <p>How long the rumble takes to swell back after each kick. The only duck timing control: the attack and the hold are fixed.</p>
        <ul><li>Short for a tight dip, long for a swell that fills the whole gap before the next kick. About 300 ms at noon.</li><li>Its CV input is where accents come from: a velocity or accent lane from the sequencer varies the swell from hit to hit.</li></ul>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">8</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Engine switch</h3><span class="range">Sub · Texture · Mutable</span></div>
        <p>Chooses the smear engine.</p>
        <ul><li><b>Dark FDN 1 (Sub):</b> an 8-line feedback delay network built for low end. Light diffusion and slow modulation keep some punch and a steady sub pitch.</li><li><b>Dark FDN 2 (Texture):</b> the same network, larger, fully diffused, with deeper modulation and a brighter loop: a moving wash that suits Out 2.</li><li><b>Mutable:</b> the plate reverb from Mutable Instruments' Rings. Brighter and familiar.</li><li>Both Dark FDNs run on one network, so switching between them mid-sound is smooth.</li></ul>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">9</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Drive type switch</h3><span class="range">Tube · Iron · Transistor</span></div>
        <p>Chooses the saturation curve for Drive, in order of aggression.</p>
        <ul><li><b>Tube:</b> asymmetric, adds even harmonics. Thickens the sub and keeps its weight.</li><li><b>Iron:</b> a transformer core. The signal is integrated to flux, the flux saturates, and the result is turned back into voltage, so the bass saturates first and the top stays clean. Compresses the fundamental and growls above it.</li><li><b>Transistor:</b> a hard clip. Odd harmonics, dense, square-ish. The industrial one.</li></ul>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">10</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Mix pos button</h3><span class="range">where Mix In joins</span></div>
        <p>Each press moves Mix In between the end of the chain and before the smear, where it is repeated, smeared and driven with the kick. The lights chase three times to say which: left to right in red for the end of the chain, right to left in blue for before the smear. It always starts at the end of the chain.</p>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">11</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Lights</h3><span class="range">four, left to right</span></div>
        <ul><li><b>Trigger:</b> a pink flash on each detected kick.</li><li><b>Hold:</b> lit while the duck is fully down, before the swell.</li><li><b>Rumble:</b> follows the rumble's volume: bright at rest, dark on the duck, fading back up with the release.</li><li><b>Predelay:</b> one pulse per kick, one Predelay interval later, when the smeared kick arrives.</li></ul>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">12</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>CV inputs</h3><span class="range">seven, one per knob</span></div>
        <p>Each adds to its knob, so a sequencer or an LFO can move release, drive or split per step. The two switches have no CV.</p>
        <p class="note"><span class="todo">To confirm</span> CV range per the Versio hardware.</p>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">13</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Gate input</h3><span class="range">unassigned</span></div>
        <p>Nothing yet. The duck fires from the kick itself, so a trigger that isn't the kick had no job here. The jack is kept free for a later use.</p>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">14</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Kick Input</h3><span class="range">In 2 · modular level</span></div>
        <p>Your kick, into In 2 (the right-hand input), on its own: the detector fires on whatever arrives here. About 5 Vpp nominal. There is no input levelling: the kick's level is the rumble's level, and a hotter kick drives harder.</p>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">15</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Mix In</h3><span class="range">In 1 · end of chain or before the smear</span></div>
        <p>A second source, into In 1. At the end of the chain (the default) it joins after Drive on each output. It isn't smeared or driven, so it stays clean. Noise is the classic choice: slow sample-and-hold for dark grit, white noise for air. Most noise sits above the split, so it lands on Out 2; only the part below the split ducks with the rumble.</p>
        <ul><li>Press Mix pos to move it before the smear: then it is repeated, smeared and driven with the kick. The smears are dark, so bright noise comes out quieter there.</li><li>Unpatched, it's silent. The kick goes into In 2, so the panel's normal (In 1 into In 2) never applies.</li><li>Bring Out 2 back in here through an external reverb or distortion and it joins the rumble, ducked, alongside the internal smear.</li></ul>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">16</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Out 1 · rumble</h3><span class="range">below the split · hot</span></div>
        <p>The rumble: everything below the split, driven, ducked, with a 30 Hz high-pass to keep the deepest sub out of the way. It runs hot, so set its level at the mixer or with an attenuator.</p>
        <ul><li>A soft clipper at the end is a safety net. With default settings it barely works; with Drive and Smear pushed up, it is the last stage of saturation.</li></ul>
      </div></article>
      <article><span class="num">17</span><div>
        <div class="hd"><h3>Out 2 · texture</h3><span class="range">above the split · hot</span></div>
        <p>The texture: everything above the split, driven, ducked by the Texture duck amount, with its own soft clipper. Use it as a second layer, or process it on its own: a high-pass, a delay, a different reverb. High-pass it above the kick's body (about 200–300 Hz) and it won't compete with the kick.</p>      </div></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ THE DUCK ============ -->
  <section id="duck" aria-labelledby="duck-h">
    <h2 id="duck-h">The duck</h2>
    <p>The kick and the rumble share the same low frequencies, so they take turns. On every kick the rumble drops out, then swells back in before the next one. That push and pull is what makes a rumble groove.</p>
