      <div class="legend"><span class="lk">kick</span><span class="lr">rumble, Out 1</span><span class="lt">texture, Out 2</span><span class="ld">duck (Out 1 volume)</span></div>
    </div>
    <h3>One envelope, three stages</h3>
    <ul class="features">
      <li><b>Attack: 2 ms</b><span>A smooth S-curve down. It hides under the kick's own transient, so it never clicks.</span></li>
      <li><b>Hold: 30 ms</b><span>Fixed. Long enough for the kick's attack to have the low end to itself, short enough that the swell starts while the kick's body is still sounding, which is where a rumble lives. A hold that followed the kick's whole body was tried: these kicks stay within 6 dB of their peak for 200–280 ms, so it held through the entire beat.</span></li>
      <li><b>Release: a mild swell</b><span>Rises evenly in loudness from the ducked level back to full, so the swell is audible all the way up instead of popping back. The Release knob sets its length; Texture duck sets how much of it Out 2 follows.</span></li>
    </ul>
    <h3>How it fires</h3>
    <p>From the kick itself, every time. The detector listens to the kick above 150 Hz, where every attack has energy and no tail does, so the previous kick's sub ringing on never hides the next one: a kick a 16th after another fires just the same. Across thirteen test kicks on four patterns, from four on the floor to a 1/16 roll, it found every hit and invented none, within 5 ms of the onset on the slowest-attack samples and under 1 ms on kicks with a click.</p>
    <p class="note">No gate, no clock. Shindo processes the kick, so a trigger that isn't the kick has nothing to do. Accents come from the Release CV instead: vary the swell per hit from a velocity lane.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ PATCH IDEAS ============ -->
  <section id="patches" aria-labelledby="patches-h">
    <h2 id="patches-h">Patch ideas</h2>
    <div class="patches">
      <div class="patch"><h3>First rumble</h3><ol><li>Kick through a stackcable: one end to your mixer, the other into the Kick Input (In 2). Out 1 to the mixer too, turned down to start.</li><li>Engine on Sub, Split at noon, Smear at noon, Diffusion at 2 o'clock.</li><li>Release around 300 ms.</li><li>Predelay up until the swell lands between kicks.</li></ol><span class="why">The classic dark rumble: one cable in, the duck fired by the kick.</span></div>
      <div class="patch"><h3>Accents</h3><ol><li>Your sequencer's velocity or accent lane into the Release CV.</li><li>Release knob around 10 o'clock, so the lane adds to it.</li></ol><span class="why">Accented kicks get a longer swell, ghost kicks a short dip: the duck shapes the groove per hit.</span></div>
      <div class="patch"><h3>Squared sub</h3><ol><li>Drive type on Transistor, Drive past 3 o'clock.</li><li>Split up toward 300 Hz, so the harmonics stay on Out 1.</li><li>Smear low, Diffusion low.</li></ol><span class="why">The rumble becomes a buzzing, squared-off sub that ducks hard on every kick. Iron instead for a compressed, growling version.</span></div>
      <div class="patch"><h3>Your own reverb</h3><ol><li>Out 2 into any reverb or distortion in the rack.</li><li>Its output back into Mix In (In 1), Mix pos at the end of the chain.</li></ol><span class="why">The external effect joins the rumble below the split, ducked on every kick, alongside the internal smear.</span></div>
      <div class="patch"><h3>Noise bed</h3><ol><li>Noise into Mix In (In 1): slow sample-and-hold for grit, white noise for air.</li><li>Set the noise level at the source, or through a VCA to modulate it.</li></ol><span class="why">The noise pumps with the rumble but stays clean, adding texture on top of the smear.</span></div>
      <div class="patch"><h3>Breathing drive</h3><ol><li>A slow LFO into the Drive CV, Drive knob around 2 o'clock.</li><li>Drive type on Tube or Iron.</li></ol><span class="why">The LFO sweeps the rumble from warm to driven and back.</span></div>
      <div class="patch"><h3>Breakdown</h3><ol><li>Smear near full, Diffusion full.</li><li>Stop the kick.</li></ol><span class="why">The rumble rings out as a drone for a few seconds, then fades by itself.</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ SIGNAL FLOW ============ -->
  <section id="flow" aria-labelledby="flow-h">
    <h2 id="flow-h">Signal flow</h2>
    <div class="flowwrap">
      <svg class="sf" viewBox="0 0 1000 930" role="img" aria-label="Signal flow. Input and trigger: the kick input splits to the rumble path and, through an onset filter above 150 Hz, to the kick detector that fires the duck; the Gate jack is unassigned. Rumble: predelay, diffusion (early taps), smear, then Split divides the sound. Duck and output: below the split, a make-up that follows Split, Drive, Mix In joining, a 30 Hz high-pass, the duck VCA at full depth and a soft clipper to Out 1; above it, Drive, Mix In joining, the duck VCA scaled by Texture duck and a soft clipper to Out 2. The duck envelope is attack, a 30 ms hold and the release swell.">
        <defs>
          <marker id="ar" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path class="arrow" d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z"/></marker>
