      </svg>
    </div>
    <p>The kick goes two ways: to the detector, and into the rumble path: predelay, Diffusion (early taps on the kick, from a few regular ones to many scattered ones), then the smear. Mix In joins after Drive, or, with the Mix pos button, alongside the kick before the predelay. Split divides the sound: below it goes to Out 1 as rumble, above it to Out 2 as texture, and each half is driven on its own by the Drive knob and type, so the sub saturates as a sub and keeps its harmonics on Out 1. The detector listens to the kick above 150 Hz, where every attack has energy and no tail does, so a kick a 16th after another still fires. Each hit pulls Out 1 all the way down for 30 ms, then the rumble swells back at the Release speed; Out 2 ducks by the Texture duck amount.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ SPECS ============ -->
  <section id="specs" aria-labelledby="specs-h">
    <h2 id="specs-h">Specs</h2>
    <div class="tablewrap"><table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Format</td><td>Eurorack, 10 HP</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Platform</td><td>Noise Engineering Versio (Electro-Smith Daisy Seed). Shindo Versio is alternate firmware: install it on a Versio module.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Audio</td><td class="mono">48 kHz</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Inputs</td><td>Kick Input (In 2), Mix In (In 1), 7 × CV. Gate: unassigned.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Outputs</td><td>Out 1 (rumble, below the split), Out 2 (texture, above it). Both hot.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Controls</td><td>7 knobs, 2 three-way switches, 1 button, 4 RGB lights</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Depth, power</td><td>As the Versio hardware. <span class="todo">To confirm</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Install</td><td>Flashed over USB, as other Versio firmware: hold BOOT, tap RESET, then flash with dfu-util or the Daisy web programmer.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
  </section>

  <footer>
    <p><b>Status:</b> proof of concept. The firmware runs on the Versio and is being tested on hardware, with a desktop sandbox running the same engine for tuning by ear. Details on this page can still change.</p>
    <p><b>Credits:</b> the Mutable engine is the reverb from Mutable Instruments' Rings by Émilie Gillet, used under the MIT license. Versio is a platform by Noise Engineering; Shindo Versio is independent firmware by Nippon Industrial Noise, not made by them.</p>
  </footer>
